    "lawCode": "Amd §§7 & 3, Chap 592 of 1998",
    "lawSection": "Parks",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6566\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the Hudson river park act, in relation\nto development or redevelopment of Pier 40 in the Hudson river park\n\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the Hudson river park act, in relation\nto development or redevelopment of Pier 40 in the Hudson river park\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to allow for the redevelopment of Pier 40 in\nthe Hudson River Park, with a wider range of potential uses than are\ncurrently permitted.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends paragraph (b) of subdivision 9 of the\nHudson River Park Act, to permit development or redevelopment of Pier 40\nto include business, professional, or governmental office space, not to\n700,000 square feet, with up to 100,000 square feet for offices and\noperations of the Hudson River Park Trust (the \"Trust\"). The maximum\nheight of any building would be 88 feet, with an additional 20 feet for\nmechanical structures, and any development or redevelopment would\nrequire at least 320,000 square feet of playing field space and a mini-\nmum footprint of 65% of the pier devoted to passive and active public\nopen space. The Trust would also be required to establishes a Pier 40\nTask Force to review any request for proposals, and any development or\nredevelopment would be subject New York City's Uniform Land Use Review\nProcedure.\n\nSection two of the bill amends Subdivision 11 of the Hudson River Park\nAct, to allow the Trust to enter into a 49 year lease on Pier 40, with a\n25 year renewal and an additional 24 year renewal.\n\nSection three of the bill amends Paragraph (vi) of Subdivision (g) of\nthe Hudson River Park Act, to allow business, professional, or govern-\nmental offices on Pier 40.\n\nSection four of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 1998, the Hudson River Park Act was enacted, creating the Hudson\nRiver Park with a legal obligation that commercial nodes within the park\ngenerate income to help fund operations and maintenance. The largest of\nthose commercial nodes, Pier 40, has a mix of allowable uses but does\nnot allow commercial office space. In the intervening decades, Pier 40\nhas been heavily used for active sports, recreational human-powered\nboating, commercial maritime uses, office space for the Trust, and park-\ning for the local community. Despite significant capital investment from\nthe City, State, and Federal governments, the Trust will needs addition\nal funding; which development or redevelopment of Pier 40 would poten-\ntially provide. The commercial office space that would be permitted by\nthis bill may facilitate development that would help the Trust meet its\nobligations for Hudson River Park.\n\nThe amendment to the Act establishes limitations on any development or\nredevelopment of Pier 40 that are intended to balance the need for addi-\ntional revenue, community concerns about the scale of any new buildings,\nand the continuing need for passive and active recreational spaces.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
